Martin Read wrote:
> Robert@shavian.fsnet.co.uk wrote:
> 
>>Deeper dungeon levels are more difficult.
> 
> 
> They are more dangerous, which is *not* the same thing.

It's close enough. Danger and difficulty are mostly correlated.

>>On the insanely hard setting, the game could add 10 to each of
>>these values, so you can get polymorph traps on level 1.
> 
> 
> And if a leocrotta spawns in your starting room (possible even at a skew
> of +5; +10 brings you the delights of liches and golden nagas), you are
> dead, dead, dead unless you're a particularly fortunate wizard.

That's what makes it insanely hard. Even a skew of +3 would be
challenging, but it would still reward skillful play.

Obviously, more sophisticated adjustments could be made - tweaking the
game for or against the player in many ways - but a straightforward
skew is a adequate starting point, if you want difficulty levels
at all.


